    94	### 2.1 What happens under the other plausible index conventions
    95	
    96	If the bound is imposed only at `j=1`, or for every `j>=1`, or on reverse monic coefficients `[pi^(N-j)]`, the following exact family disproves emptiness:
    97	
    98	```text
    99	P = pi^N + (c/2)*gamma^2,   Q = pi,   N>=1,
   100	Jac(P,Q)=c*gamma.
   101	```
   102	
   103	Both polynomials are monic in pi. For the reverse convention, the constant coefficient has allowed degree `3N`, and degree two fits. These solutions are excluded by the full forward convention precisely at `j=0`.
   104	
   105	The entire monic affine-in-pi slice can be classified. Write `P=pi+B(gamma)` and `Q=pi+D(gamma)`. Then `Jac=c*gamma` if and only if
   106	
   107	```text
   108	D = B-(c/2)*gamma^2+d,    d in K.                         (W2)
   109	```
   110	
   111	For the positive-coefficients-only bound, `B` is arbitrary. For the reverse bound at pi-degree one, require `deg B<=3`. This is a complete classification of that slice, not merely an example.
   112	
   113	Without monicity, write `P=A*pi+B`, `Q=C*pi+D`. The pi coefficient of the Jacobian is `A'*C-A*C'`. Its vanishing makes the nonzero pair `A,C` proportional over `K`. After an invertible constant linear target change, write the pair as `(A*pi+B,E(gamma))`. Its equation is `-A*E'=c'*gamma`, so the only possibilities are
   114	
   115	```text
   116	(a*pi+B,       -(c'/(2a))*gamma^2+e),
   117	(a*gamma*pi+B, -(c'/a)*gamma+e),   a!=0.                  (W3)
   118	```
