   130	## 3. Moh descent supplies a shifted Laurent support condition
   131	
   132	On a licensed source branch, put `u=u_s`, `v=v_s`, `ell=v-u-1`, and retain the root truncation. Moh's substitution is
   133	
   134	```text
   135	theta=y^(-1), gamma=theta^(1/u), z=y-b*x-e,
   136	sigma=A(gamma)+pi*gamma^v,
   137	y=gamma^(-u),
   138	x=b^(-1)*(gamma^(-u)-e-A(gamma)-pi*gamma^v).              (D1)
   139	```
   140	
   141	The finite sum `A` comes from the common inversion coefficients. For a safe finite chart it suffices to allow every exponent `0,...,v-1`; this enlarges the actual truncated sum and does not assert that every coefficient choice is realized. The source hypothesis gives polynomiality of the descended pair, monicity, and pi-degrees `n'=nu/d_s`, `m'=mu/d_s`, with `d_s=u+v` on the present cohort. The proposition gives
   142	
   143	```text
   144	Jac_gamma,pi(gbar(sigma),Tbar_1(sigma))
   145	       = -(u/b)*gamma^(v-u-1).                          (D2)
   146	```
   147	
   148	Direct differentiation verifies the sign: `x_pi=-gamma^v/b`, `y_gamma=-u*gamma^(-u-1)`, and `y_pi=0`; therefore `Jac(x,y)=-(u/b)*gamma^ell`. The derivatives of `A` cancel from this determinant. They do not disappear from the descended polynomial coefficients.
   149	
   150	Indeed, for `h(y,z)=sum h_ar*y^a*z^r`, its literal substitution has pi coefficient
   151	
   152	```text
   153	[pi^j]h(gamma^(-u),A+pi*gamma^v)
   154	 = gamma^(v*j) * sum_(a>=0,r>=j) binom(r,j)*h_ar
   155	                              *gamma^(-u*a)*A^(r-j).    (D3)
   156	```
   157	
   158	The surviving factor `A^(r-j)` invalidates the proposed argument that source polynomiality forces degree at most `v*j`. Negative gamma coefficients must cancel to obtain a polynomial; positive coefficients supplied by `A` need not vanish.
   159	
   160	The true elementary support statement is obtained after a Laurent shear:
   161	
   162	```text
   163	D_tilde(gamma,pi)=D(gamma,pi-A(gamma)*gamma^(-v))
   164	                =h(gamma^(-u),pi*gamma^v).
   165	```
   166	
   167	Every exponent `(i,j)` in this Laurent polynomial satisfies `i<=v*j` and `i congruent to v*j mod u`; negative `i` are allowed. The shear has determinant one but can destroy polynomiality in gamma. It is not the receiver in Theorem 1. For example `P=gamma^(-1)`, `Q=-c*gamma^3*pi` satisfy this Laurent wedge and have Jacobian `c*gamma`; the divisibility proof of Theorem 1 does not apply.
   168	
   169	An explicit polynomiality control makes the failure visible. Set `u=1,v=3,A=gamma+gamma^2`, and in the source ring define
   170	
   171	```text
   172	F=y^3*z-y^2-y,  H=y*z-1,  G=F^2+H.
   173	```
   174	
   175	Then `(F,H,G)` descend exactly to
   176	
   177	```text
   178	(pi, gamma+gamma^2*pi, pi^2+gamma^2*pi+gamma).
   179	```
   180	
   181	The last polynomial is monic in pi and has the forbidden constant coefficient gamma. This is a counterexample to the asserted inference from polynomiality and monicity. It is not asserted to satisfy every Keller/source hypothesis, and is not a counterexample to the Jacobian conjecture. The stronger K16 control in §5 verifies the support problem on an actual reconstructed cone family.
   182	
   183	A necessary but deliberately generous unshifted support rectangle is enough for an honest atlas. If the original polynomial has total degree at most `N` and `deg A<v`, its substitution has gamma exponents between `-uN` and `vN`, and pi-degree at most `N`. On a licensed branch, its negative gamma coefficients and its coefficients above the descended pi-degree vanish. These are precisely the extra source equations retained next.
