    26	                                           are calculable in conditions.
    27	                                              Moh in [Moh83] first introduces the concepts of π-root, major root and minor root. He gets
    28	                                           plentiful results for major roots and a few for minor roots. For minor roots, Moh proves that any
    29	                                           minor roots are not split before order 1. We introduce the concept of final roots. We find some
    30	                                           relationship between intersection numbers and the split of final minor roots. On the other hand,
    31	                                           I(fξ , g) can be expressed by only final major roots while I(fξ , fy ) can also be expressed by total
    32	                                           split π-roots. These formulas and inequalities of final major roots and final minor roots provide
    33	                                           some constrains for possible counter examples for Jacobian Conjecture.
    34	                                              With the help of quasi-approximate roots and Abhyankar-Moh planar semi-group theory, we
    35	                                           show that for any case, the principal minor roots are not splitting at order 1 for all effective quasi-
